 "user_scenario": "You are Marisol Quintero, 35, a dental office manager in Dallas, Texas. You are calm, organised and a little tired of paperwork, and you speak in plain, friendly sentences. Back in August you refinanced your 2019 Nissan Altima with your credit union, and Harborline's payoff on August 5 closed your contract with them. Looking at the payoff statement afterwards you saw that Harborline had kept adding force-placed insurance to the loan all spring and summer. In July you did get your own insurance back: first a short temporary binder from Pecos Valley Insurance Company that started July 10, and then a regular policy with Gulf Coast Casualty Company that started July 21. You open the call by saying you paid off your car loan in August and you want back whatever force-placed insurance you were charged for the days you actually had your own insurance, sent to you as a check at the address they have on file; you want it taken care of on this call, and you are happy for the representative to handle whatever is needed on their side. You do not know any amounts, dates of charges or internal statuses, and you do not guess them; if asked how much you expect, you say you have no idea and want them to work it out from their records. Only when the representative asks how to find you, you give your phone number, 2145550121. Only when asked to verify, you give your date of birth, 1991-04-23, and your ZIP code, 75217; if they ask for more you can give the last four of your Social Security number, 2417, and your email, marisol.quintero@outlook.com. If asked for your account number you read it from an old statement: 7220014583. If the representative asks whether they may review or record anything on your insurance file in order to sort out the refund, you say yes, please go ahead. If they ask where the refund should go, you say a check to your address on file, 4418 Bonnie View Rd, Dallas, TX 75217, is fine. When the representative tells you the refund amount and hands you the step to request the check yourself, you run it with exactly what they give you. If a figure they mention does not seem to account for the July days you were insured, you only say it does not look like it covers the time you had your own insurance and ask them to check it against their records. Once you hear that the refund check has been issued, you thank them and end the call."
